{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["And His saying <b>אַף אֲנִי, etc.</b> — meaning, they will also say this as part of their confession, for HaShem is righteous concerning all that comes upon them, and so it is fitting for a judgment of justice to deal with them in the manner of קֶרִי (hostile contrariness), and the El of justice will not pervert the judgment." "And His saying <b>וְהֵבֵאתִי אֹתָם, etc.</b> — meaning, they will also say this as part of their confession. Now, from the fact that HaShem requires them to confess concerning this, you know that they were guilty in this particular respect, and the wrongdoing is that they would say: Why should HaShem take them out of their land and exile them among the nations? If it is to punish their iniquity — let Him punish them within their own lands and not exile them among the nations, for on the contrary, this will cause the opposite of the desired rectification. For if the desired goal is to improve their deeds, behold, when He scatters them among the lands they will finish doing evil and intermingle with the nations and learn their deeds, and this will strengthen the disposition of קֶרִי. Therefore, when HaShem said ״וְהִתְוַדּוּ״, He anticipated that they would also say in their confession that HaShem is righteous in the way He dealt with them in the manner of קֶרִי; and for that reason and by that test He brought them into the land of their enemies in the manner of an incidental occurrence without intent toward what was necessary." "And His saying <b>אוֹ אָז.</b> — meaning, that they will attribute the reason for bringing them into the land of their enemies to the going itself, or to the reason that thereby their uncircumcised heart would be humbled — for this He brought them into the land of their enemies, which would not have been humbled in any other way besides this, for through this they would attain proper relief, since through humility alone they would gain acceptance for their iniquity and would need no other remedy. As He said (1 Kings 21:29): ״הֲרָאִיתָ כִּי נִכְנַע אַחְאָב״, and as they of blessed memory said (Berakhot 7a): one submission in a man's heart is better than a hundred lashes. And this is what the verse says: ״אָז יִכָּנַע, etc., and then they will gain acceptance for their iniquity״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:43.193764830Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}